condition_name: Acute Bronchitis
condition_slug: acute-bronchitis
source: NSTG 2022
introduction: "An inflammation of the bronchial tubes. Commonly caused by a variety of viruses, same as those that are responsible for common cold. Primary bacterial aetiology may also occur. Acute bronchitis can last from a few days to 10 days but the associated cough may last for several weeks after the infection has cleared up. Bronchitis lasting up to 90 days is still usually classified as acute bronchitis."
clinical_features[1]:
  - type: Clinical Features
    features[8]: Cough,Sputum production,"Sputum may be clear, yellow or greenish",Wheezing,Muscle and backache,Low grade fever,Shortness of breath in severe cases,Chest pain especially while coughing
investigations[4]: Chest x-ray,Sputum tests,Quality sputum for culture and tests for evidence of allergy,Pulmonary function tests
treatment:
  goals[0]:
  non_drug[0]:
  drug[3]: Antibiotics - Amoxicillin 500 mg PO 8 hourly for 5 – 7 days,Macrolide e.g. erythromycin 500 mg 8 hourly 5 – 7 days,Co-trimoxazole 960mg 12 hourly 5 – 7 days
  adverse_reactions_and_cautions[1]: "Cotrimoxazole: Nausea, Skin rashes, rarely Stevens-Johnson syndrome"
  supportive_measures[0]:
differential_diagnoses[4]: Cough-variant asthma,Mycoplasma pneumonia,Chlamydia pneumonia,Bordetella pertussis
complications[3]: Pneumonia,Acute respiratory failure,Repeated bouts of acute bronchitis over time may lead to COP
prevention[0]:
other_investigations: null
definitive_treatment: null
prognosis: null